  <abstract xml:id="abstract">
      <p>
          Drag-and-drop assessment systems typically retain event logs that are richer
          than the final submitted answer but are not immediately suitable for analysis.
          This article introduces a state-space framework for representing drag-and-drop
          responses as timestamped trajectories and a source-informed pipeline for
          reconstructing those trajectories from incremental interaction logs.
      </p>

      <p>
          The framework combines explicit item-source alignment, deterministic event
          replay, check-anchor validation, attempt segmentation, provenance tracking,
          and quality-control classifications.  It also defines a restrained family of
          baseline process features describing response distance, movement efficiency,
          checking behavior, error profiles, and timing.
      </p>

      <p>
          We evaluate the framework using [NUMBER] students, [NUMBER] drag-and-drop
          items, [NUMBER] attempts, and [NUMBER] logged events from [COURSE OR SETTING].
          Reconstructed states agree with authoritative check records in [PERCENT] of
          eligible cases, and [PERCENT] of attempts satisfy the prespecified criteria
          for full analysis readiness.  Sensitivity analyses show that [MAIN ROBUSTNESS
          RESULT].
      </p>

      <p>
          The reconstructed trajectories reveal [ONE-SENTENCE EMPIRICAL RESULT SHOWING
          INFORMATION BEYOND FINAL CORRECTNESS].  These results establish a reproducible
          method for transforming low-level drag-and-drop logs into validated educational
          process data while making reconstruction uncertainty and analytic eligibility
          explicit.
      </p>
  </abstract>

</frontmatter>
